\import{macros}

\title{2026-W31}
\date{2026-07-27}

\p{Publish wxmp posts through the year on working with agents. I see agents as partners to be ensouled, and look for the work style that unleashes their full potential, following the image of our own nature: making and using tools, learning and changing the world.}

\p{On the technical side, the ground runs from sandboxing agents while giving them controlled access to resources, coordinating their work, and a knowledge base greatly enriched beyond the LLM Wiki idea, up to loop engineering, which keeps agents autonomous while facilitating humans in staying well-informed and steering at will, and to the design discussions for building our own harness for recursive self-improvements.}

\p{On the relational side, impacts on the value of our work and on ourselves as individuals and teams are weighed from several angles: how supercharged individuals collaborate through agents; value judged increasingly by agents as much as by people, with carbon-based sentients bringing warmth and silicon-based agents addressing scarcity; craft and judgement about logic, value, and taste staying priceless; and humans growing stronger and more creative in the process of working with, and being augmented by, agents.}






\p{Start developing math notes on five subjects:}

\p{\code{fgap} works the foundational gap between established mathematical tools and open questions about physical structures beyond the Standard Model, currently a small, source-grounded vertical slice through Hamilton's quaternions and the quaternionic realisation of the binary tetrahedral group. See also [fgap](https://github.com/utensil/fgap).}

\p{\code{fcap} asks whether Lean can be both the formal language for Clifford algebras and the implementation language for efficient symbolic and numerical computation, the aim being a library whose foundations and executable implementations are both verified, still at spike-test stage, with an appendix that reads the Tau Ceti Clifford and Spin roadmaps as context and possible tools. See also [fcap](https://github.com/utensil/fcap).}

\p{\code{connes} treats the OpenAI, Zhou, and Anthropic counterexamples to Connes' rigidity conjecture as three formalization targets, following Zhou in detail, with remarks comparing the approaches and what the formalization taught. See also [connes-rigidity](https://github.com/utensil/connes-rigidity).}

\p{\code{ftip} weighs the potential and limits of machine intelligence against the contribution advantage sentients keep in a civilization, especially conceptual discovery and the insightful breakthrough whose cost may be prohibitive for machines, examining (post-)training objectives and techniques, model architectures, agent procedures and their retained state, and the economy of learning.}

\p{\code{fdip} takes the finite structure as the building blocks and the continuum as an emergent large-scale description, so that geometry, time, and matter are induced rather than postulated, with the remaining parameters following from symmetry, from the initial conditions of a specific bubble universe, and from the local background.}

\p{Start contributing to four Tau Ceti roadmaps:}

\p{\strong{SpinRepresentations} is the coverage ladder from Clifford identities and filtration through bivectors to spinor modules, real Clifford algebras, exceptional isomorphisms, and triality, its notes kept in an appendix of the fcap notes, and the Lean 4 skill polished along the way, see [my Lean 4 skills](https://github.com/utensil/formal-land/tree/dev/lean-skills/lean4/skills), [the roadmap thread](https://leanprover.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/610393-Tau-Ceti/topic/Roadmap.3A.20RepresentationTheory.2FSpinRepresentations), and [the route map](https://utensil.github.io/formal-land/spinrep/spinrep-route-map.html).}

\p{\strong{OrthogonalSpinGroups} covers orthogonal groups and the vectors inside a Clifford algebra, the Pin and Spin actions with their norms and comparisons, explicit lifts, and the real forms in low dimensions, see [the route map](https://utensil.github.io/formal-land/spinrep/spingrp-route-map.html).}

\p{\strong{GeometricTopology} walks from manifolds with boundary and handle attachment up to triangulations and PL structures, aiming at the resolved problems of Kirby's list, see [the route map](https://utensil.github.io/formal-land/geotopo/geotopo-route-map.html).}

\p{\strong{DifferentialGeometry} takes forms and the exterior derivative through flows, de Rham cohomology, degree, planar Green, and the Riemannian calculus, begun from an interest in geometric calculus, see [the route map](https://utensil.github.io/formal-land/dg/dg-route-map.html).}

